  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 116 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSatellite shows a broad upper low over the Desert Southwest with a\ncouple of embedded vorticity maximums circulating around it, the \nmost pronounced one in southwest Arizona. This latter feature \nwill end up being the main low which will drift slowly northeast \ninto eastern Utah on Sunday. Ahead of that, weak lift will be \npresent both from the QG aspect and an upper level speed max \nahead of this trough. One wave of energy is currently organizing \nwith a considerable amount of convection in southwest and south\ncentral Colorado, and that will arrive late this afternoon/ \nevening as it advects north/northeast in the mean flow. While our \ninstability is marginal and S/SW flow is generally downslope for \nus, the earlier frontal passage has brought deepening upslope to \nabout 11,000 ft MSL east of the Front Range. That will help offset\nthe substantial drying we've seen in this flow pattern over the \nlast couple days, and thus showers will have a greater chance of \nsurviving as they roll off the higher terrain and onto the plains \nand I-25 Corridor. We've bumped PoPs up just a tad thanks to the \nupslope component. We still think most precipitation amounts will \nbe light (0.10-0.20 inch or less), although the Mosquito Range and\nsouth facing mountains along the Summit County/Park County line \nincluding Hoosier Pass could easily pick up 3-5\" of snow with some\ntravel impacts through this evening. Snow amounts tonight would \ntaper off rather quickly along/north of I-70 to just an inch or \nso. One other location that stands a higher chance (30-60%) of \nmore meaningful precipitation near 0.5 inch rain would be over the\nfar eastern plains, roughly east of a line from Holyoke to Limon.\n\nOn Sunday and Sunday night, synoptic scale lift will gradually \nincrease as the upper level low moves east across the forecast\narea. Models were still highly variable, with the synoptic scale \nmodels still pointing toward better chances of more widespread \nshowers than today. However, we will be fighting a deeper\ndownslope component than today, as flow just off the deck is more\nsoutherly. The only upslope component would be quite shallow and\nnorth of a Denver Cyclone. Thus, the highest chances of rain late\nSunday afternoon and especially Sunday night will likely shift \ntoward the northern half of the forecast area. Probabilities of \n>0.50\" of rain are 30-50% near the Wyoming/Nebraska border, with \nmuch lower probabilities farther south. In the mountains, snow \nlevels will likely hover near 9,000-10,000 feet which would \nsupport a few more inches of snow for the high country. Again, \nthat favors the northern tier of mountains so another 1-5\" is \nexpected there, but some high end solutions still show a small \nchance (10-15%) of as much as 8-10\".\n\nBy Monday morning, there is good agreement that the upper trough\nwill be moving east and northeast of the forecast area, taking the\nprecipitation with it. Temperatures will start to moderate with\nMonday's highs pushing back into the 60s for the lower \nelevations. We'll turn to a more zonal flow Tuesday which means \nfurther warming and mostly dry weather, although a couple of the \nensemble members have just enough moisture for a few rain/snow \nshowers in the northern mountains. \n\nWednesday through Friday are shaping up to be dry and warmer as\nsouthwest flow aloft and flat ridging develops across the \nforecast area. Highs could push back to near 70F as ensembles  \ncontinue to trend to a warmer solution. \n\nWe're still watching the next potential trough and/or return of\nsubtropical moisture as we head toward next weekend. While there\nis considerable uncertainty in the details, it does appear we are\nshaping up for more unsettled weather but again quite a bit of\nblocking by the southern CO mountains.",
